Z||000000||fiero56||12-31-2000||12:32 AM||||Got a problem here.<P>The folks have a computer they bought earlier this summer. I needed to install a (IDE)ZIP drive of mine in their computer(I'm on my notebook right now), so I opened the case, noticed they didn't have any free bays open, so I just hooked it up and left it "hanging" for the few minutes I needed it. I got my files, shut down the computer, removed the ZIP drive, put everything back togeher, and powered the computer back up. Problem is I can't get past POST. It shoots me a 5 sec beep every couple of seconds, and I don't know what brand BIOS is in there to decode it. There is no video, so no help there. This is a computer they bought from a local shop. Turns out it is an Intel Celeron 366 overclocked to 533(socket 370 with passive and active cooling). Means the board isn't an Intel, but to find out the brand of most components in there, I will need to run the FCC ID number. Any thoughts what might be hanging me up. I have triple checked all connections in there, made sure I didn't disturb any jumpers, and checked all memory connections.<P>I don't know where to start if I don't know what code its shooting at me. Any help or thoughts?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||12-31-2000||12:36 AM||||You've knocked something loose or worse zapped it with static while it was open. Shut it off and unplug it. let it sit a couple minutes while you check all the connectors. then try it again. <P>Most of these newer computers get really sh_tty about you working on them while plugged in. You can cook the brain board doing that.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-31-2000||12:46 AM||||I always unplug them before taking the case off. I used an ESD strap too since I don't like taking chances at frying stuff. I'm worried I fried something though. I'll run through it again.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||12-31-2000||01:15 AM||||Well, all looks fine from the outside. My guess is something got smacked with static. Probably happened when the cat came and rubbed my legs under the desk when I didn't know she had come in the room. All the shops will be closed until Tuesday. I'll have to drop it off and have them check everything out, since I don't have anything else with a 370 socket to check the chip. My guess is the board or CPU. We'll find out Tuesday I guess.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroSTETZ||12-31-2000||02:22 AM||||wait- first, open the case, (while its off) then look for the watch battery (cmos battery) un plug the battery, and stick it in to the battery holder sideways making contact on both of the contacts, then put the battery back in, and boot the computer. i do this kinda stuff for CompUSA ( i am a technician) email me to let me know what happens. (btw im actually at work right now, every night after the store closes, we play half life TFC!!!)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||12-31-2000||05:29 AM||||If it's a Celeron with a Slotket converter, reseat the Slotket - these things pop loose quite easily. Also, reseat the memory. To be honest, I don't think you zapped anything.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||12-31-2000||05:30 AM||||PS: if that doesn't work, try reseating all plug-in boards as well.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||12-31-2000||09:41 AM||||While I was in the case, I reseated all cables, removed all memory and reseated it. I also took off the massive heat sinks, and reseated the processor. But I may have also scraped the board removing the heat sink clips. Everything bad that could possibly happen running through my head right now.<P>Cliff, thanks, but no slot-kets on this computer. Its a PPGA package plugging directly to the board.<P>Since I don't have any documentation for this computer(literally no documentation), I don't want to chance losing settings I won't be able to properly restore. What does removing the battery help with this problem(other than resetting CMOS settings)? Just curious, don't want to sound like an a$$, but couldn't find a way to word the question with a more friendly tone.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||12-31-2000||11:14 AM||||Next step I would do is to set the speed back to 366. Find the jumpers for the bus speed and set it back to 66Mhz. I don't think the multiplier jumper has been changed (since Celerons are multiplier-locked) but check and see that they are set to 5.5x. If you don't know which jumpers they are, check the motherboard - it's usually written on the motherboard itself. If it's one of those jumperless motherboards, then you need to switch it off/on for 5 times or so. It will set the speed back to default.<P>Also, like I said earlier, reseat the plug-in boards. If one of them is not seated properly, your system will not start.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||12-31-2000||12:18 PM||||The only plug in board on it is the PCI 56K modem. It would have more stuff on it, but it only has one PCI slot and one ISA slot, both of which use the same expansion port on the case.<P>The board uses jumpers, I'll look around and try to locate the bus jumpers. Like you said the Celeron is multipier locked, as well as most Intel boards, so I'm not even sure what board this thing has in it yet.||||reg||1
Z||000010||baldlobo||12-31-2000||06:01 PM||||If it's o/ced then it might have just cooked it's self.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||LarryB||01-01-2001||12:01 PM||||This is going to be a bit long-winded so bear with me.<P>It sounds like this motherboard has an integrated video card, not one in a PCI or AGP slot.<P>The motherboard uses the diagnostic beeps to try to tell you what the problem is; the number and type of beeps identify the type of problem.  That's why you usually hear 1 short beep when the computer starts to post - that means it passes diagnostics.<P>A repeating series of long beeps almost always means a memory problem but could be a problem with the video electronics, or even something unrelated.  Here are several steps you can try, after each step power up the computer and see if it helps. If not, move on to the next step.<P>Be sure to unplug the power before each step and use proper ESD techniques. <P>1) If you can find the CMOS jumper, jumper it for 5-10 seconds to the "clear data" position (its normally in the Keep Data position so its obvious how to rejumper it) then put it back to the Keep Data position. If you can't find it, do exactly what FieroSTETZ recommended removing the internal battery.  Just be sure that the power cable is unplugged before doing this or it won't work.  <P>2) Remove the memory, inspect the memory and memory sockets for damage or debris, then reseat it.  You can also come back to this step later and reseat the memory in a different slot. Motherboards with less than 4 memory slots usually have less buffering circuitry (helps cut the manufacturers cost) and can be touchy about memory placement.<P>3) Disconnect all cabling from the motherboard except for the keyboard, mouse, monitor cable, power cable, CPU fan, and case connectors like power switch, speaker, etc. (this means disconnect hard drive and floppy cables, printer cable, etc.) Remove any cards you've got in an expansion slot, except if you in fact don't have an integrated video card, in that case keep just the video card in place. In effect, strip down the motherboard to the bare minimum needed to get the computer to post.<BR>If you get to this point and it still doesn't work, you can leave the computer like this and retry steps 1) and 2).  If that doesn't work figure on bringing the computer in to be looked at.<P>6 months is way too soon for an 366MHz  Celeron @550MHz to kill itself, as long as it was being cooled properly.   It could be that the vendor used generic PC66 memory which gave up the ghost after running for 6 months @ 100MHz.  It's not as likely the motherboard died. <P>Let us know what you find and hope for the best. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by LarryB (edited 01-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroSTETZ||01-01-2001||01:07 PM||||well, at work i work on mostly factory assembled machines, and about 50% of the time, when a computer won't boot and its a software clocked machine, resetting the bios clears the settings, resets the clock speed to like 366mhz (66mhz bus for the memory) and gets rid of all the extraneous crap||||reg||1
Z||000013||LarryB||01-01-2001||03:57 PM||||I'd go along with that. That's why your recommendation was first on the list.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroSTETZ:<BR><B>well, at work i work on mostly factory assembled machines, and about 50% of the time, when a computer won't boot and its a software clocked machine, resetting the bios clears the settings, resets the clock speed to like 366mhz (66mhz bus for the memory) and gets rid of all the extraneous crap</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
